                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "016ab9ff-e43d-33f4-834d-e400fa76dd8b",
                        "entry_id": "0d3268e2-f151-306c-b459-7a0dd8b475ce",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of Crimean Khanate: A historical country in Crimean Peninsula, Europe. The Crimean Khanate, which for much of its history controlled most of the Crimean peninsula and various adjacent areas of the mainland."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "29a6a286-798d-4448-9005-6e27dd2d8df7",
                                "sense_id": "016ab9ff-e43d-33f4-834d-e400fa76dd8b",
                                "text": "According to Zimin, this individual was in the service of Prince Fedor Vasili'evich Riazanskii as early as 1498, two years after his service on the embassy to the Crimea."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "77bff3b2-914f-40a6-af83-6784a916b0c1",
                                "sense_id": "016ab9ff-e43d-33f4-834d-e400fa76dd8b",
                                "text": "On its return journey in 1633 the Tatar embassy was arrested by the Muscovite government, which was already at war with the Crimea, and only after several months was it released as a result of an appeal by Sweden[.]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e0c6a7de-d492-43be-97fb-50cd1a3e856c",
                                "sense_id": "016ab9ff-e43d-33f4-834d-e400fa76dd8b",
                                "text": "As late as 1665, the Crimea's demand for continued and increased tribue from Moscow was one of the conditions leading to a peace treaty between a khan and tsar („kaznu v Krym prodolzhat' prisylat' s pribyl'iu“). Notwithstanding Moscow's rhetorical exercises, the Russian government was acutely aware that as long as Russia's military force remained ineffective against the Crimea, peace with Crimea could only be purchased."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
